    <h2>Picking a Game Engine</h2>
    <p>
      A game engine is a software toolkit that gives you pre-built components like physics engines, sound players, and rendering systems, so you do not have to write everything from scratch. Here are 21 popular game engines to explore:
    </p>

    <div class="engine-grid">
      {engines.map((e) => (
        <div class="engine-card">
          <h4>{e.name}</h4>
          <p>{e.desc}</p>
          <a href={e.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="engine-link">
            Download {e.name} ↗
          </a>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p>
      Looking for in-depth comparisons and platform matrices? Check out our dedicated <a href={`${base}equipment/game-engines/`}>Game Engines Directory</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Completing Your First Tiny Project</h2>
    <p>
      New game creators often try to build massive dream games right away, get overwhelmed, and give up. Finishing small projects builds skills and boosts your confidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Joining a game jam</strong> is one of the best ways to complete your first game. Game jams are friendly community events where creators build a playable game from scratch in 48 to 72 hours around a surprise theme. The strict time limit forces you to make decisions quickly and finish a complete project.
    </p>
    <p>
      Another great strategy is <strong>remaking classic arcade games</strong> like Pong, Snake, or Space Invaders. Because the design rules are already written, you can focus purely on learning how your engine handles inputs, movement, collisions, and user interface scores.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Limiting your project to a single mechanic</strong> is another proven method. Pick one action—like swinging a grappling hook or flipping gravity—and build a complete 3-level game around that single input.
    </p>

    <h2>Joining the Game Development Community</h2>
    <p>
      Game development is easier when you do not do it alone. Connecting with other creators gives you access to advice, instant bug fixes, playtest feedback, and encouragement.
    </p>
    <p>
      Popular places to join include dedicated GameDev Discord channels, engine forums, and Reddit communities like <code>r/gamedev</code>. You can also share progress updates on platforms like itch.io or YouTube using public devlogs. Sharing early screenshots and clip updates helps you build an audience while getting constructive advice on your mechanics.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout-tip">
      <strong>Next Quest:</strong> Ready to turn raw concepts into captivating systems? Continue to <a href={`${base}quest-logs/game-design-101/`}>Game Design 101: Crafting the Experience →</a>
    </div>

  </WikiLayout>
</BaseLayout>
